::GEO_U04_L03_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L03_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.3
Question:
Two triangles have angle A ≅ angle D and angle B ≅ angle E. Which criterion justifies similarity?
{
=A. AA similarity#Two congruent angle pairs are enough to prove triangle similarity.
~B. SSS similarity#SSS similarity requires all three side ratios.
~C. SAS similarity#SAS similarity requires two proportional side pairs and the included angle.
~D. HL congruence#HL proves congruence in right triangles, not similarity here.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L03_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.3
Question:
Corresponding sides of two triangles are 3 and 6, 5 and 10, and 7 and 14. Which criterion applies?
{
~A. AA similarity#No angle information is given.
=B. SSS similarity#All three corresponding side ratios are 2, so SSS similarity applies.
~C. SAS similarity#The evidence gives all three side ratios, not two sides and an included angle.
~D. CPCTC#CPCTC is used after congruence, not to establish similarity.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L03_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.3
Question:
Two triangles have a congruent included angle and adjacent side ratios both equal 4. Which criterion applies?
{
~A. AA similarity#Only one angle pair is described.
~B. SSS similarity#Only two side ratios are described.
=C. SAS similarity#SAS similarity uses two proportional side pairs and the congruent included angle.
~D. SSS congruence#The side lengths are proportional, not necessarily equal.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L03_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.3
Question:
If triangle ABC ∼ triangle DEF, which angle corresponds to angle B?
{
~A. angle D#A corresponds to D, not B.
~B. angle F#C corresponds to F, not B.
~C. angle A#This is an angle in the first triangle, not the matching angle in the second.
=D. angle E#The order of the similarity statement maps A to D, B to E, and C to F.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L03_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.3
Question:
Two triangles have side ratios 2, 2, and 3. Is SSS similarity justified?
{
=A. No, all three side ratios must be equal.#SSS similarity requires the same ratio for every corresponding side pair.
~B. Yes, two ratios match.#All three ratios must match, not only two.
~C. Yes, any ratio greater than 1 works.#The actual ratio value matters less than equality across all pairs.
~D. No, side ratios are never used.#SSS similarity is based on side ratios.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q06::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L03_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.3
Question:
Which evidence is enough for AA similarity?
{
~A. One pair of sides is congruent.#One side pair alone does not establish similarity.
=B. Two pairs of corresponding angles are congruent.#Two angle pairs establish triangle similarity because the third angle pair must also match.
~C. One side ratio is known.#One ratio alone is insufficient.
~D. The triangles are drawn with the same orientation.#Orientation is not a similarity criterion.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q07::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L03_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.3
Question:
Which similarity statement matches A to R, B to S, and C to T?
{
~A. triangle ABC ∼ triangle RTS#This pairs B with T instead of S.
~B. triangle ABC ∼ triangle SRT#This pairs A with S instead of R.
=C. triangle ABC ∼ triangle RST#The vertex order correctly pairs A-R, B-S, and C-T.
~D. triangle ABC ∼ triangle TSR#This reverses the intended correspondence.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q08::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L03_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.3
Question:
A dilation maps one triangle to a triangle congruent to another. What conclusion is supported?
{
~A. The triangles have no relationship.#The transformation sequence gives a relationship.
~B. The original triangle must be larger.#Dilation may enlarge or reduce depending on scale factor.
~C. Only one angle is related.#A dilation preserves all angle measures.
=D. The original triangle is similar to the other triangle.#Dilation supports similarity, and congruence-preserving motion can complete the mapping.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q09::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L03_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.3
Question:
Two triangles have sides 6, 8, 10 and 9, 12, 15. Which statement is true?
{
=A. They are similar by SSS similarity.#Each side in the second triangle is 1.5 times the corresponding side in the first.
~B. They are congruent by SSS.#The side lengths are not equal, so congruence is not shown.
~C. They cannot be similar because sizes differ.#Similar triangles may have different sizes.
~D. They are similar by AA only.#The given evidence is side ratios, not angle pairs.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q10::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L03_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.3
Question:
A pair of triangles has one congruent angle but no side information. What can be concluded?
{
~A. AA similarity applies.#AA needs two angle pairs.
=B. Similarity is not justified yet.#One angle pair alone is not enough for a triangle similarity criterion.
~C. SSS similarity applies.#SSS needs three side ratios.
~D. SAS similarity applies.#SAS needs two side ratios and the included angle.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q11::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L03_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.3
Question:
In SAS similarity, which angle must be congruent?
{
~A. Any angle in either triangle#The angle must be between the proportional sides.
~B. The largest angle only#The criterion depends on included position, not size.
=C. The included angle between the proportional sides#SAS similarity uses the angle included by the two proportional side pairs.
~D. The right angle only#SAS similarity applies beyond right triangles.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q12::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L03_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.3
Question:
Triangles have side ratios 4/8, 5/10, and 6/12. What criterion is supported?
{
~A. AA similarity#No angle pairs are provided.
~B. SAS similarity#The evidence gives all three sides, not an included angle.
~C. No similarity#The ratios are equal, so similarity is supported.
=D. SSS similarity#All three ratios equal ½, so SSS similarity is supported.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q13::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L03_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.3
Question:
Two triangles have angle M ≅ angle P and angle N ≅ angle Q. What is the correct corresponding order?
{
=A. triangle MNO ∼ triangle PQR#M maps to P, N maps to Q, so O maps to R.
~B. triangle MNO ∼ triangle QPR#M would map to Q, which conflicts with the given correspondence.
~C. triangle MNO ∼ triangle RQP#M would map to R, which is not given.
~D. triangle MNO ∼ triangle PRQ#N would map to R instead of Q.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q14::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L03_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.3
Question:
Which statement correctly uses transformation language for similarity?
{
~A. Only translations can prove similarity.#Translations alone preserve size and show congruence, not non-congruent similarity.
=B. A dilation and rigid motion can map one triangle to the other.#Similarity can be justified by a dilation combined with rigid motions.
~C. Reflections always change size.#Reflections preserve size.
~D. Rotations destroy angle measures.#Rotations preserve angle measures.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q15::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L03_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.3
Question:
If two triangles are similar, what is true about corresponding angles?
{
~A. They are supplementary.#Supplementary angles sum to 180°, which is not required for all corresponding angles.
~B. They are always right angles.#Similar triangles may or may not be right triangles.
=C. They are congruent.#Similar triangles have congruent corresponding angles.
~D. They cannot be compared.#Correspondence allows angle comparison.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q16::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L03_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.3
Question:
If two triangles are similar, what is true about corresponding side lengths?
{
~A. They are always equal.#Equal side lengths would show congruence, a special case of similarity.
~B. They are unrelated.#Similarity creates a proportional relationship.
~C. They must differ by 1.#Similarity depends on ratios, not fixed differences.
=D. They are proportional.#Similar triangles have equal ratios of corresponding side lengths.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q17::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L03_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.3
Question:
A student says two triangles are similar because one side pair has ratio 2. What is the problem?
{
=A. One side ratio is not enough evidence.#Similarity requires a complete criterion such as AA, SAS, or SSS.
~B. The ratio must be less than 1.#Scale factors may be greater than 1.
~C. Side ratios cannot support similarity.#Side ratios can support similarity when enough corresponding sides are known.
~D. The triangles must be congruent first.#Similarity does not require prior congruence.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q18::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L03_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.3
Question:
Which triangle similarity criterion uses all three side pairs?
{
~A. AA similarity#AA uses angle pairs.
=B. SSS similarity#SSS similarity checks all three corresponding side ratios.
~C. SAS similarity#SAS uses two side pairs and the included angle.
~D. HL similarity#HL is a congruence criterion for right triangles.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q19::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L03_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.3
Question:
Which triangle similarity criterion uses two angle pairs?
{
~A. SSS similarity#SSS uses three side ratios.
~B. SAS similarity#SAS uses side ratios and an included angle.
=C. AA similarity#AA similarity uses two pairs of congruent corresponding angles.
~D. CPCTC#CPCTC is not a similarity criterion.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q20::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L03_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.3
Question:
Which triangle similarity criterion uses two side ratios and one included angle?
{
~A. AA similarity#AA uses two angle pairs.
~B. SSS similarity#SSS uses all three side ratios.
~C. ASA congruence#ASA is a congruence criterion, not this similarity criterion.
=D. SAS similarity#SAS similarity matches two proportional sides with the included congruent angle.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q21::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L03_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.3
Question:
If triangle JKL ∼ triangle PQR, which side corresponds to KL?
{
=A. QR#The statement maps J to P, K to Q, and L to R, so KL maps to QR.
~B. PQ#JK corresponds to PQ.
~C. PR#JL corresponds to PR.
~D. JK#JK is a side in the first triangle, not the corresponding side in the second.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q22::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L03_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.3
Question:
If triangle RST ∼ triangle XYZ, which side corresponds to RS?
{
~A. YZ#ST maps to YZ.
=B. XY#R maps to X and S maps to Y, so RS maps to XY.
~C. XZ#RT maps to XZ.
~D. ST#ST is a different side in the first triangle.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q23::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L03_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.3
Question:
Two triangles have side ratios 1.2, 1.2, and 1.2. Which conclusion follows?
{
~A. The triangles are congruent.#A ratio of 1.2 changes size, so congruence is not shown.
~B. Only two sides are proportional.#All three listed ratios match.
=C. The triangles are similar by SSS similarity.#All three corresponding side ratios are equal.
~D. The triangles are unrelated.#Equal ratios provide similarity evidence.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q24::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L03_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.3
Question:
Two triangles have two proportional side pairs but the angle given is not included. Can SAS similarity be used?
{
~A. Yes, any angle works.#Position matters for SAS similarity.
~B. Yes, because two side ratios are enough.#Two side ratios alone are not enough for SAS.
~C. No, because side ratios never matter.#Side ratios matter, but the included angle condition is missing.
=D. No, the angle must be included.#SAS similarity requires the congruent angle between the proportional sides.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_Q25::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L03_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.3
Question:
Which explanation is strongest?
{
=A. The triangles are similar by AA because two corresponding angle pairs are congruent.#This names both the evidence and the correct criterion.
~B. The triangles look similar.#Appearance is not enough evidence.
~C. The triangles are probably similar.#A proof needs specific evidence.
~D. The triangles have labels.#Labels alone do not establish similarity.
}


